WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.590
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:10.110
This work is doing something truly remarkable:
if we have a piece of audio of a real person

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:15.900
speaking, and a target video footage, it will
retime and change the video so that the target

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.800
person appears to be uttering these words.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.360
Whoa!

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.000
This is different from what we've seen a few
episodes ago, where scientists at NVIDIA worked

00:01:02.019 --> 00:01:08.170
on synthesizing lip sync geometry for digital
characters solely relying on audio footage.

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:11.040
The results were quite amazing, have 
a look.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.600
This was great for animating digital characters
when all we have is sound.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:32.469
But this time around, we're interested in
reanimating the footage or real, existing

00:01:32.469 --> 00:01:33.469
people.

00:01:33.469 --> 00:01:38.180
A prerequisite to do this with a learning
algorithm is to have a ton of data to train

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:42.869
on - which we have in our possession as there
are many hours of footage of the former president

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:45.539
speaking during his weekly address.

00:01:45.539 --> 00:01:48.590
This is done using a recurrent neural network.

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:53.609
Recurrent neural networks are learning algorithms
where the inputs and outputs can be sequences

00:01:53.609 --> 00:01:54.609
of data.

00:01:54.609 --> 00:01:59.399
So here, in the first part, the input can
be a piece of audio with the person saying

00:01:59.399 --> 00:02:03.960
something, and it is able to synthesize the
appropriate mouth shapes and their evolution

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.159
over time to match the audio.

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:11.150
The next step is creating an actual mouth
texture from this rough shape that comes from

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:15.620
the learning algorithm, which is then used
as an input to the synthesizer.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:19.910
Furthermore, the algorithm is also endowed
with an additional pose matching module to

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:25.500
make sure that the synthesized mouth texture
aligns with the posture of the head properly.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:30.370
The final retiming step makes sure that the
head motions follow the speech correctly.

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:34.760
If you have any doubts whether this is required,
here are some results with and without the

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:36.480
retiming step.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.440
You can see that this indeed substantially
enhances the realism of the final footage.

00:03:01.519 --> 00:03:06.870
Even better, when combined with Google DeepMind's
WaveNet, given enough training data, we could

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:12.620
skip the audio footage altogether and just
write a piece of text, making Obama, or someone

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:22.189
else say what we've written.

00:03:22.189 --> 00:03:30.739
There are also a ton of other details to be
worked out, for instance, there are cases

00:03:30.739 --> 00:03:36.160
where the mouth moves before the person starts
to speak, which is to be taken into consideration.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:39.920
The dreaded "umm"-s and "ahh"-s are classical
examples of that

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.440
There is also an important jaw correction
step and more.

00:03:57.459 --> 00:04:01.840
This is a brilliant piece of work with many
non-trivial decisions that are described in

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:05.550
the paper - make sure to have a look at it
for details, as always, there is a link to

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:07.549
it is available the video description.

00:04:07.549 --> 00:04:12.519
The results are also compared to the Face2face
paper from last year that we also covered

00:04:12.519 --> 00:04:13.519
in the series.

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:19.609
It is absolutely insane to see this rate of
progress over the lapse of only one year.

